---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const seoTitle = 'Game Porting Toolkit Without the Terminal: Play Windows Games on Mac in One Click';
const description =
  "Set up optional Apple D3DMetal in Pixel Port with your own Game Porting Toolkit 3.0 download. Requirements, import steps, and game-specific limits.";

const canonical = new URL('/game-porting-toolkit', Astro.site).toString();
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Game Porting Toolkit without the terminal',
      description,
      url: canonical,
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Pixel Port' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL('/', Astro.site).toString() },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Game Porting Toolkit', item: canonical },
      ],
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is Game Porting Toolkit for players or developers?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: "Developers. Apple built the Game Porting Toolkit so game studios could evaluate porting their Windows games to macOS. Installing it yourself means Xcode command line tools, Homebrew, terminal commands, and manual per-game setup. It was never designed as a consumer product.",
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'How do I get Game Porting Toolkit results without using the terminal?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Pixel Port offers guided Apple D3DMetal setup on macOS 15 or later with Rosetta 2. It prepares its compatible engine; you download Game Porting Toolkit 3.0 from Apple under Apple\'s terms and import the disk image locally. D3DMetal is experimental, and compatibility varies by game.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<Base title={seoTitle} description={description}>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} slot="head" />

  <article class="wrap py-16 sm:py-24">
    <nav class="text-sm text-muted">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-fg">Home</a> <span class="px-1">/</span> Game Porting Toolkit
    </nav>

    <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Game Porting Toolkit, without the terminal
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted">
      When Apple shipped the Game Porting Toolkit, it proved something important: Windows games can
      run remarkably well on Apple Silicon. Then everyone opened the install guide and found Xcode,
      Homebrew, and a wall of terminal commands. That is because it was built for game developers,
      not for you.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <a
        href="/download"
        class="inline-block rounded-full bg-linear-to-r from-accent to-accent-2 px-7 py-3.5 font-semibold text-white shadow-[var(--shadow-glow)] [text-shadow:0_1px_2px_rgba(10,8,22,0.4)] transition-[transform,filter] hover:brightness-110 active:scale-[0.98]"
      >
        Download Pixel Port
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-invert mt-14 max-w-2xl prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-accent-2">
      <h2>What the toolkit actually is</h2>
      <p>
        The Game Porting Toolkit is Apple's evaluation environment for studios considering a Mac
        port. It translates Windows DirectX graphics calls to Metal so a developer can see how
        their game behaves on Apple Silicon. It works (often impressively) but using it yourself
        means installing developer tooling, compiling dependencies, wrangling Wine prefixes, and
        repeating the setup dance for every single game. Miss a step and you get a black screen
        and no explanation.
      </p>

      <h2>Set up optional D3DMetal in Pixel Port</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Port 0.5.1 offers guided Apple D3DMetal setup under your account menu →
        Settings → Prism Runtime. On macOS 15 or later with Rosetta 2, Pixel Port prepares
        its compatible engine. You obtain Game Porting Toolkit 3.0 from Apple under Apple's
        terms, then import the disk image locally. Apple's download is a separate step.
      </p>
      <p>
        Follow the <a href="/docs/getting-started/#optional-apple-d3dmetal-setup">D3DMetal
        setup guide</a> for the exact download and import steps. D3DMetal is experimental;
        a Ready setup does not guarantee a particular game's compatibility. Legacy Prism
        remains the recommended starting point, and Prism Next remains Upcoming.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before installing, read your game's current label, notes, and requirements in
        the <a href="/games">catalogue</a>. Results can vary by Mac, runtime, game version,
        and mode. Some games have an established blocker,
        including <a href="/games/anti-cheat">kernel-level anti-cheat</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 rounded-3xl border border-accent/25 bg-linear-to-br from-accent/15 to-accent-2/10 p-10 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">Choose your game. Follow guided setup.</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-lg text-muted">
        Built for Apple Silicon, with setup instructions and game-specific limits.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/download"
        class="mt-7 inline-block rounded-full bg-linear-to-r from-accent to-accent-2 px-8 py-4 text-lg font-semibold text-white shadow-[var(--shadow-glow)] [text-shadow:0_1px_2px_rgba(10,8,22,0.4)] transition-[transform,filter] hover:brightness-110 active:scale-[0.98]"
      >
        Download for Mac
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
